CSF 2.0 Core data (data/csf-core.json)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) 2.0 and its Core (Functions, Categories,
Subcategory outcome statements, and Implementation Examples) are works of the
U.S. Government and are in the public domain. The data file shipped here was
generated from the official machine-readable export of the NIST Cybersecurity
and Privacy Reference Tool (CPRT): https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cprt

The generation is reproducible from the official source with:
    npm run build-csf-core
See scripts/build-csf-core.js for the exact extraction (it keeps the 22 CSF 2.0
Categories and drops withdrawn CSF v1.1 entries and "[Withdrawn]" placeholders,
yielding the 106 live Subcategory outcomes, each with its official NIST
Implementation Examples). Per NIST, Implementation Examples are illustrative and
not exhaustive; they are not a baseline of required actions.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Informative-reference crosswalks (data/csf-crosswalks.json)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The crosswalk data relates CSF 2.0 Functions, Categories and Subcategories to
identifiers in other frameworks. The relationships are those published through
NIST's National Online Informative References (OLIR) Program
(https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/olir) and were retrieved from the NIST CSF 2.0
reference data service. Each relationship is kept at the level its mapping
records it. Each mapping was developed by the party named as its developer in
NIST's catalog of informative references
(https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/olir/informative-reference-catalog); that name
and the mapping's release date are recorded with it. Inclusion of a mapping is
not an endorsement by NIST or by this project.

A mapping whose developer is neither NIST nor the publisher of the referenced
framework is marked as a third-party mapping. By default the deliverables carry
only mappings developed by NIST or by the body that publishes the mapped
framework. Third-party mappings are available but must be chosen deliberately,
and they are reproduced as published — including entries that are non-specific
or appear erroneous.

Links are limited to documents hosted by NIST. A mapping's own document is
linked only when NIST hosts it; otherwise NIST's catalog is the citation. The
data therefore carries no links to third-party pages, which can move or change.

The file contains identifiers only (control, requirement, or clause numbers).
It reproduces no control or requirement text and no control titles from any
referenced framework. The referenced frameworks remain the property of their
respective publishers and are identified here by name and by reference only;
consult each publisher's own edition for their content. A mapping records that
a CSF element and a reference are related. It does not state that they are
equivalent, and it is not a determination of conformance with the referenced
framework.

The generation is reproducible from the official source with:
    npm run build-crosswalks
See scripts/build-crosswalks.js for the exact selection and extraction (which
mappings are shipped and why, the integrity checks against data/csf-core.json,
and how identifiers are normalized).

